# libXfont2 -- the X server's font engine, and the package that decides whether
# Xwayland starts at all.
#
# THE THING WORTH KNOWING BEFORE READING THE FLAGS. Xwayland calls
# SetDefaultFont("fixed") during startup and FatalError()s when it fails
# (dix/main.c 229-231) -- it does not degrade, it exits. Nothing in this tree
# installs a single font file into /usr/share/fonts/X11/misc or any of the five
# other directories Xwayland's default font path names, so on paper every
# Xwayland start should die there.
#
# It does not, because of --enable-builtins. libXfont2 compiles a gzipped PCF of
# 6x13 and of the cursor font into its own .text segment (src/builtins/fonts.c),
# registers them as a font path element literally named "built-ins", and
# dix/dixfonts.c:1716 APPENDS that element to every font path the server is
# given. src/builtins/fonts.c:1243 aliases "fixed" onto it.
#
# So the entire core-X-font story on this distribution is one compile-time
# option in this package, its failure mode is the server exiting at startup
# rather than a missing glyph, and the option defaults to enabled -- which is
# exactly the shape that gets turned off by someone tidying up a build. It is
# stated below and asserted in install.sh, twice, on two different artefacts.
#
# Not an LFS package, so it is pinned here rather than in versions.env.
SRC_URL=https://www.x.org/pub/individual/lib/libXfont2-2.0.7.tar.xz
SRC_SHA256=8b7b82fdeba48769b69433e8e3fbb984a5f6bf368b0d5f47abeec49de3e58efb
SRC_DIR=libXfont2-2.0.7
#
# --enable-builtins        see above. THE ONE THAT MATTERS.
#
# --enable-freetype        the scalable-font backend, and where PCF and BDF are
#                          actually rasterised. Default enabled; stated because
#                          disabling it takes XFONT_FONTFILE with it
#                          (configure.ac 118) and the builtin PCF above would
#                          then have no rasteriser to go through.
#
# --enable-pcfformat       the format the builtin fonts ARE. Turning this off
#                          leaves --enable-builtins compiled in and unusable.
# --enable-bdfformat       BDF, the source form of the same bitmap fonts.
#
# --disable-snfformat      already the default. SNF is the pre-1990 bitmap
#                          format; nothing has shipped one this century.
#
# --enable-fc              connections to an xfs font server. Kept at its
#                          default rather than switched off: nothing here runs
#                          xfs, so this is dead code, but it is dead code that
#                          costs one static function table, and disabling it
#                          changes what an X11 client can put in its font path.
#                          Stated so that it is a decision rather than a
#                          default nobody read.
#
# --without-bzip2          already the default, and the reason is finding-10
# shaped: X_BZIP2_FONT_COMPRESSION is a DECODER. Enabling it would make this
# package link libbz2 to read a form of font file nothing in this tree ships.
#
# --disable-devel-docs, --without-xmlto, --without-fop: the DocBook chain.
# XORG_WITH_XMLTO and XORG_WITH_FOP default to auto and would switch themselves
# on in an image that happened to carry the tools, which is the same class as
# libnotify's -Dman=true reaching for a stylesheet by URL. Named so the answer
# does not depend on the image.
CONFIGURE_ARGS="--disable-static \
--enable-builtins --enable-freetype --enable-pcfformat --enable-bdfformat \
--disable-snfformat --enable-fc --without-bzip2 \
--disable-devel-docs --without-xmlto --without-fop"
